# Model (JPA Entities)

Model classes are `@Entity` annotated classes that represent database tables. They hold both data and business behavior related to their own state.

## Structure

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String customerName;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false)
    private OrderStatus status;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

    protected Order() {}

    public Order(String customerName) {
        this.customerName = customerName;
        this.status = OrderStatus.DRAFT;
    }

    public void confirm() {
        if (this.items.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot confirm an empty order");
        }
        this.status = OrderStatus.CONFIRMED;
    }

    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        if (this.status != OrderStatus.DRAFT) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot add items to a non-draft order");
        }
        this.items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this);
    }

    // Getters

    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public String getCustomerName() { return customerName; }
    public OrderStatus getStatus() { return status; }
    public List<OrderItem> getItems() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(items); }
}
```

## Identity

- Use `Long` with `@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)` for auto-increment IDs
- `@Id` marks the primary key

```java
// ✅ GOOD — Auto-generated ID
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;
```

## Constructors

- **Protected no-arg constructor**: Required by JPA (can be `protected`)
- **Public constructor(s)**: For creating valid instances with required fields
- Validate invariants in the public constructor

```java
@Entity
public class Product {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private double price;

    protected Product() {}

    public Product(String name, double price) {
        if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Product name cannot be empty");
        }
        if (price < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Price cannot be negative");
        }
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public double getPrice() { return price; }
}
```

## Behavior Over Setters

Prefer methods that express business intent over generic setters. Use setters only for fields that genuinely need unrestricted updates.

```java
// ⚠️ WORSE — Anemic model with setter
public void setStatus(String status) {
    this.status = status;
}

// ✅ BETTER — Business behavior
public void confirm() {
    if (this.status != OrderStatus.DRAFT) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Only draft orders can be confirmed");
    }
    this.status = OrderStatus.CONFIRMED;
}
```

## Relationships

Use JPA annotations for entity relationships:

```java
// One-to-Many
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

// Many-to-One
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "order_id", nullable = false)
private Order order;

// Many-to-Many
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(
    name = "student_courses",
    joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "student_id"),
    inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "course_id")
)
private Set<Course> courses = new HashSet<>();
```

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### ❌ I will NEVER:
1. Expose mutable collections directly (return `Collections.unmodifiableList()`)
2. Allow invalid state through constructors (validate required fields)
3. Put service-layer logic in entities (no repository calls, no HTTP)
4. Skip the protected no-arg constructor (JPA requires it)

### ✅ I will ALWAYS:
1. Use `@Entity` and proper JPA annotations
2. Validate required fields in the public constructor
3. Prefer business methods over setters for state changes
4. Provide a protected no-arg constructor for JPA
5. Use `Long` with `@GeneratedValue` for IDs
